From: winvm runtime <noreply@stuffbucket.co>
Subject: [PATCH] hvf: keep ROM devices read-only when dirty logging stops

When migration (savevm, migrate) stops dirty logging, hvf_log_stop() gives
every logged section read, write and execute access back. That includes a ROM
device in romd mode, such as the pflash holding UEFI variables, which
hvf_set_phys_mem() maps read-only so that writes trap to the device. After
any save of a running guest, the guest's flash command writes land in the
backing RAM instead; the device stays in read-array mode, and edk2's NOR flash
driver polls the status register forever on its next variable write (seen as
a hang in firmware on the next reboot).

Restore write access only where hvf_set_phys_mem() granted it.

Carried by winvm-runtime until upstream QEMU has an equivalent fix.
---
--- a/accel/hvf/hvf-all.c
+++ b/accel/hvf/hvf-all.c
@@ -149,7 +149,13 @@
                          MemoryRegionSection *section, int old, int new)
 {
     assert(old != 0);
-    if (new == 0) {
+    /*
+     * Give write access back only where hvf_set_phys_mem() granted it. A ROM
+     * device (pflash in romd mode) must stay read-only so the guest's writes
+     * keep trapping to the device; otherwise its commands land in the
+     * backing RAM and the device never sees them.
+     */
+    if (new == 0 && !section->mr->readonly && !section->mr->rom_device) {
         hvf_unprotect_dirty_range(section->offset_within_address_space,
                                   int128_get64(section->size));
     }
